																	<div class="b-c b-text-c b-s b-s-t60 b-s-b60 b-cs cf"><h1 style="text-align:left" class="wnd-align-center"><strong>Kidney Tissue</strong></h1> <p><strong>Normal kidney anatomy and histology allows the kidney to conduct its function of waste removal from the bloodstream through renal ultrafiltration and selective reabsorption.</strong></p><p>The labelled kidney section, on the left, is stained with Hematoxylin and Eosin (H&amp;E), a routine histological stain.&nbsp;</p><p>The section shows the microscopic structures that facilitates kidney physiology and function.</p><p><strong><br></strong></p><p>Learning points include:</p><ul><li>Identification of the key histological or microscopic structures of the normal kidney, such as:</li></ul><p>1. Blood vessels (For blood flow to the kidney nephron for removal of waste)</p><p>2. Glomerulus (Encompassed by the Bowman's capsule, is the start of the kidney nephron, where renal ultrafiltration occurs)</p><p>3. Renal tubules (Where absorption and excretion of various substances occur at)</p><p><br></p><ul><li>Some technical information:</li></ul><p>1. Identifying suitable stains for kidney specimens</p><p>2. Finding out the stain used in a given section</p><p>3. Understanding and pointing out procedural errors in specimen preparation, processing and staining</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p> </div>
																</div>
															</div>
														</div>
													</div>
												</div><div class="b b-text cf">
													<div class="b-c b-text-c b-s b-s-t60 b-s-b60 b-cs cf"><p>Figure 1.&nbsp;</p><ul><li>H&amp;E Stains - Collagen fibres and Nuclei</li><li>Highlights normal kidney structure</li></ul><p>1. Can be used to determine kidney tubule damage</p><p><br></p></div>
												</div><div class="b b-text cf">
													<div class="b-c b-text-c b-s b-s-t60 b-s-b60 b-cs cf"><p>Figure 2.&nbsp;</p><ul><li>MT (Masson's Trichrome) stain - Collagen fibres, Nuclei, Cytoplasm and Erythrocytes. (especially glomerulus and its cellular components)</li><li>Highlights fibrosis, no matter interstitial or glomerular</li></ul><p><br></p></div>
												</div><div class="b b-text cf">
													<div class="b-c b-text-c b-s b-s-t60 b-s-b60 b-cs cf"><p>Figure 3.&nbsp;</p><ul><li>Reticulin stain - Reticulin fibres and Basement membrane</li><li>Highlights glomerular and basement membranes</li></ul></div>
												</div><div class="b b-text cf">
													<div class="b-c b-text-c b-s b-s-t60 b-s-b60 b-cs cf"><p>Figure 4.</p><ul><li>PAS (Periodic-acid-Shiff) stain - Cytoplasm, Basement Membrane, Nuclei and Fungi</li><li>Highlights glomerular and basement membranes</li><li>For identification of kidney tumors and diagnosis of other kidney pathologies</li></ul></div>
												</div>
											</div>
